/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  bubble01_reaction_mass_boiling_point -- the BOILING POINT OF A REACTION
  MASS, which is what the thermal-safety methodology takes as the technical
  limit MTT of an open vessel at atmospheric pressure.

  The mass is the one in tutorials/batch/reactor/batch26_cooling_failure
  (and batch27/batch28, the same reaction dosed): the FICTITIOUS reactants
  A and B, their product P and the inert solvent S.  constant/ is a COPY of
  batch26's sealed constant/ (the same four authored records under the same
  manifest, the same thermoPhysPropDict; only the reactor's own
  constant/reactions is left out, because no property operation reads it),
  so the boiling point below is computed on exactly the thermodynamics the
  reactor case declares: an IDEAL liquid (Raoult, gamma = 1) over an ideal
  gas, at the reactor's pressure, 1.013 bar.

  The composition is the reactor's at batch26's shipped cooling failure
  (t_f = 600 s; mole fractions of the trajectory row at 600 s, 39.3 % of B
  converted).  The process-safety EduTool rewrites the four lines with the
  reactor contents at the failure instant the reader chooses, read from the
  engine's own trajectory, and reads T_bubble back.  ONE KEY PER LINE: the
  GUI's override anchors on a key at the start of a line, so a composition
  written on one line is a knob that silently misses.

  WHAT THIS IS AND IS NOT.  The mixture is ideal BY DEFINITION (the
  substances are fictitious and the case declares it so), so it has no
  azeotrope: its bubble point lies between the normal boiling points of
  the components present -- here between B's (345.01 K) and P's
  (479.99 K), and much nearer the light end, because the volatile
  components dominate the vapour pressure.  It is a bubble point at the
  failure instant: as a runaway converts the mass, its composition, and so
  its boiling point, moves -- upward here, as the light reactants become
  the heavy product.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

operations
(
    {
        name        reactionMassBubblePoint;
        type        propertyPoint;
        state
        {
            T       330 K;          // T_p; the bubble point does not depend on it
            P       1.013 bar;      // the reactor's pressure (batch26 0/internalStates/reactor)
            composition
            {
                reactantA       0.131756705;
                reactantB       0.131756705;
                productP        0.085304119;
                solventS        0.651182471;
            }
        }
        properties  ( T_bubble );
    }
);
